1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,706
But there is something better than swapping, and it comes from an old research result.

2
00:00:05,706 --> 00:00:09,985
When a model runs, its neurons do not all fire equally.

3
00:00:09,985 --> 00:00:13,789
A small fraction fire for almost every single word.

4
00:00:13,789 --> 00:00:17,592
The large majority only fire for particular inputs.

5
00:00:17,592 --> 00:00:21,396
The researchers called them hot neurons and cold neurons.

6
00:00:21,396 --> 00:00:26,151
The insight is obvious once you hear it. Keep the hot part on the fast hardware,

7
00:00:26,151 --> 00:00:29,955
and let the cold part live in slower memory.

8
00:00:29,955 --> 00:00:33,758
Modern mixture of experts models are that idea, shipped.

9
00:00:33,758 --> 00:00:38,513
The model is split into many specialist sections, and only a few are used per word.

10
00:00:38,513 --> 00:00:43,268
So we place them by hand. The always-active parts go on the graphics card.

11
00:00:43,268 --> 00:00:47,072
The rarely-used experts go into ordinary system memory.

12
00:00:47,072 --> 00:00:52,777
The result is dramatic. A dense 27 billion model at 4-bit takes about 17 gigabytes,

13
00:00:52,777 --> 00:00:57,057
which fills a 16 gigabyte card completely, with nothing left over.

14
00:00:57,057 --> 00:01:01,336
A mixture of experts model of similar quality runs in 5 to 7 gigabytes.

15
00:01:01,336 --> 00:01:07,042
So the real move is not picking a smaller model. It is going from dense to mixture of experts.

16
00:01:07,042 --> 00:01:12,747
And at 5 to 7 gigabytes each, you can suddenly hold three models at the same time

17
00:01:12,747 --> 00:01:15,600
and stop swapping altogether.
